/*
 * todo_pdp_sir_contact_network.pli
 * Standard SIR epidemiology model using Probabilistic / PDP P systems.
 * P-system type: Probabilistic / PDP (Population Dynamics P systems)
 * Category: Epidemiology / mathematical biology
 * Complexity: L2 (intermediate)
 *
 * The SIR (Susceptible-Infected-Recovered) compartmental model is the
 * foundational framework for epidemic modeling.  In this P-system encoding,
 * individuals are represented as objects S, I, R in a single membrane.
 * The mass-action contact assumption is captured by the rule [S, I --> I, I],
 * which fires with probability beta when a susceptible individual (S) and an
 * infected individual (I) are simultaneously present in the membrane.
 *
 * Compartments:
 *   S  =  Susceptible (can be infected)
 *   I  =  Infected and infectious
 *   R  =  Recovered and immune
 *
 * Rules:
 *   [S, I --> I, I]'1  :: beta    infection (S meets I -> both become I)
 *   [I   --> R    ]'1  :: gamma   recovery  (I recovers and becomes immune)
 *
 * Initial state (population of 1000):
 *   S*990  (990 susceptibles)
 *   I*10   (10 initial infected)
 *   R*0    (no recovered initially)
 *
 * Parameters:
 *   beta  = 0.30  per-contact transmission rate
 *   gamma = 0.10  recovery rate (mean infectious period ~ 1/gamma steps)
 *
 * Expected epidemic dynamics:
 *   - Basic reproduction number R0 = beta/gamma = 3.0 > 1: epidemic takes off.
 *   - Infected population rises to a peak then falls as susceptibles are depleted.
 *   - Final recovered fraction satisfies the implicit equation 1-r = exp(-R0*r).
 *   - With R0=3, approximately 94% of the population becomes infected.
 *   - Epidemic ends when susceptibles fall below the herd immunity threshold.
 *
 * Simulation notes:
 *   Run psim with the -r flag (randomized) for stochastic simulation.
 *   Increasing beta or decreasing gamma raises R0 and accelerates the epidemic.
 *   Setting beta < gamma gives R0 < 1 and the epidemic dies out without spreading.
 *
 * Reference:
 *   Kermack, W.O. and McKendrick, A.G. (1927).
 *   "A contribution to the mathematical theory of epidemics."
 *   Proc. Royal Society of London A, 115(772), 700-721.
 */
@model<probabilistic>
@include "pdp_model.pli"

def main()
{
  /* Single membrane representing the well-mixed population */
  @mu = []'1;

  /* Initial population: 990 susceptible, 10 infected, 0 recovered */
  @ms(1) = S*990, I*10;

  /* Infection: a susceptible contacts an infected individual -> both infected */
  [S, I --> I, I]'1 :: beta;

  /* Recovery: an infected individual recovers and gains immunity */
  [I --> R]'1 :: gamma;
}

/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
/* Model parameters                                                    */
/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */

/* Transmission rate (per S-I contact per step) */
beta = 0.30;

/* Recovery rate (1/mean infectious period) */
gamma = 0.10;
